      { label: 'Doesn\'t understand GDP', detail: 'You hear "the economy grew 2.5%" and have no idea if that\'s good or bad. You can\'t evaluate whether a country is actually prosperous or just large. You confuse nominal growth with real growth and miss that inflation is eroding the numbers. You can\'t tell a recession is coming even when the data screams it.' },
      { label: 'Understands GDP', detail: 'You can evaluate any country\'s economic health in seconds. You know GDP per capita matters more than total GDP (Luxembourg > China per person). You distinguish nominal vs. real growth to see through inflation. You recognize recession signals early and make better career and investment decisions during downturns.' },
      {
        withoutVisual: { type: 'flow', title: 'Fooled by the Headline Number', nodes: [{ label: 'Nominal GDP', value: 'Reads "+5%", celebrates', color: '#EF4444', sprite: 'rocket', explain: 'The +5% is real, it is just unfinished. It counts dollars spent, and the dollar total can rise because the country made more things or because each thing got more expensive, and this number cannot tell you which.' }, { label: 'Inflation', value: 'Ignores the 7% running underneath', color: '#EF4444', sprite: 'inflation', explain: 'Prices climbing 7% would push the dollar total up 7% even if the country produced nothing extra. That is more than the entire 5% the headline was bragging about, so the whole gain and then some was just price.' }, { label: 'Real GDP', value: 'Actually -2%: the economy shrank', color: '#EF4444', sprite: 'bear', explain: '5 minus 7 leaves negative 2, so less was actually made this year than last. Two quarters in a row of a number like this is the textbook definition of a recession, and whoever cheered the headline is the last to find out.' }], footer: 'Thinks the economy is booming while it quietly contracts', sentiment: 'negative' },
        withVisual: { type: 'flow', title: 'Reads GDP Like a Pro', nodes: [{ label: 'Nominal GDP', value: 'Starts with the "+5%" headline', color: '#10B981', sprite: 'price-tag', explain: 'The exact same headline the person on the other panel read. Nominal GDP is not a trick, it is raw material, and the only difference between these two people is what they do with it next.' }, { label: 'Inflation', value: 'Subtracts the 2.5% price effect', color: '#10B981', sprite: 'calculator', explain: 'Prices rose 2.5% here, so that slice of the dollar increase bought nothing new and gets taken out. It is the same price data the Fed was watching in the last lesson when it decided whether to raise rates.' }, { label: 'Real GDP', value: '+2.5%: the true growth rate', color: '#10B981', sprite: 'check-badge', explain: '5 minus 2.5 leaves 2.5% of genuine extra output: more cars built, more haircuts cut, more software written. That is about what a healthy year looks like in a rich country, and it is the figure economists mean when they say the economy grew.' }], footer: 'Spots a recession two quarters before the crowd', sentiment: 'positive' }
      }
