        { term: 'Core Inflation', definition: 'CPI excluding volatile food and energy prices. Shows the underlying trend. The Fed watches this number most closely.', example: 'In 2022, headline CPI hit 9.1% but core was 5.9%. The gap showed that energy prices (gas at $5/gallon) were driving much of the spike.' },
        { term: 'Real vs. Nominal', definition: 'Nominal values aren\'t adjusted for inflation. Real values are. Real = Nominal minus Inflation. Always think in real terms.', example: 'A 5% raise sounds great, but if inflation is 6%, your real raise is -1%. You can buy LESS than before despite a higher salary.' },
        { term: '2% Inflation Target', definition: 'The rate most central banks aim for, high enough to lubricate the economy, low enough to preserve purchasing power.', example: 'At 2% inflation, prices double every 36 years. At 10%, they double every 7 years. The difference is enormous over a lifetime.' }
