    cq('If CPI rises from 280 to 294 over one year, what is the annual inflation rate?',
      ['4%, dividing the 14-point rise by a rounded base of 350','5%, (294-280)/280 = 14/280 = 5%','14%, using the raw point change without dividing by the base','2.8%, dividing the rise by the new CPI of 294 instead'],
      1,'Inflation rate = (New CPI - Old CPI) / Old CPI = (294-280)/280 = 14/280 = 5.0%. This is the standard formula for calculating inflation from CPI data.'),
    cq('Why do central banks target 2% inflation instead of 0%?',
      ['0% would be the better target but is simply impossible to hit in practice','2% lubricates the economy, it encourages spending, allows real wage adjustments, and gives central banks room to cut rates during recessions. 0% risks deflation.','2% is chosen mainly because it quietly shrinks the real value of government debt','It is just tradition, with no real economic reason behind the specific number'],
      1,'2% inflation is the "Goldilocks" zone: high enough to prevent deflation, encourage economic activity, and give the Fed room to cut rates (you can\'t cut below 0% easily), but low enough that purchasing power degrades slowly. At 2%, prices double every 36 years, manageable.'),
    cq('Japan experienced deflation for ~30 years. Why was this so damaging?',
      ['Falling prices sound nice but cause consumers to delay purchases, businesses to lose revenue, wages to stagnate, and the economy to enter a self-reinforcing downward spiral','Deflation only hurts exporters and leaves the average household completely unaffected','Japan\'s deflation was actually beneficial because it made all goods steadily cheaper','Deflation in Japan eventually flipped into runaway hyperinflation instead'],
      0,'The deflationary spiral: prices fall, consumers wait to buy, businesses earn less, they cut jobs and wages, people have less money to spend, prices fall further. Japan\'s GDP grew just 0.7%/year for 30 years while the U.S. grew ~3%. The stock market didn\'t recover its 1989 peak until 2024, a 34-year wait.'),
    cq('In 2022, U.S. inflation hit 9.1%. Which combination of factors caused it?',
      ['Only the supply chain disruptions caused by the COVID pandemic','Only the roughly $5 trillion of government stimulus spending','A combination: ~$5 trillion in stimulus (demand surge) + broken supply chains (supply shortage) + Russia-Ukraine war (energy/food price spike)','Only the Russia-Ukraine war and the energy price spike it triggered'],
      2,'It was a "perfect storm" of demand-pull AND cost-push inflation. Too much money ($5T stimulus) chasing too few goods (supply chain breakdowns) plus an energy shock (Russia-Ukraine war). No single factor caused 9.1%, it was all three converging simultaneously.'),
    cq('Your portfolio returned 12% last year while inflation was 4%. What was your real return, and why does it matter?',
      ['12%, since inflation has no effect on the return your portfolio earned','8%, real return = nominal (12%) minus inflation (4%). It matters because only real returns measure your actual increase in purchasing power.','16%, found by adding the inflation rate on top of the nominal return','4%, since the only part of the return you truly keep is the inflation rate'],
      1,'Real return = 12% - 4% = 8%. This means your purchasing power grew by 8%, not 12%. If you only looked at the nominal return, you\'d overestimate your wealth growth by 50%. Always calculate real returns, they tell you how much MORE you can actually buy.')
