            { label: 'Gradually reduce the deficit through a combination of spending restraint and targeted revenue increases, build fiscal space for the next crisis', consequence: 'Textbook counter-cyclical policy. During good times, reduce deficits to: (1) slow debt accumulation, (2) create "fiscal space" for the next recession, and (3) demonstrate credibility to bond markets. Even modest deficit reduction (from $1.5T to $800B) would improve the trajectory significantly.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Implement immediate, severe austerity, slash spending 30% and raise taxes sharply', consequence: 'Too extreme. Suddenly cutting 30% of spending and raising taxes would shock the economy into recession, undoing your recovery. Gradual, predictable fiscal consolidation is effective. Sudden austerity during ANY economic condition is disruptive. The goal is a glide path to sustainability, not a cliff.', score: 1 }
