        { date: '1980s-1990s', event: 'Export-oriented manufacturing takes off, China becomes the "world\'s factory"', context: 'Cheap labor (wages 1/50th of U.S. levels), massive infrastructure investment (highways, ports, power grids), and WTO accession (2001) made China the world\'s top exporter. FDI flooded in, Apple, Nike, Toyota all built supply chains in China. Manufacturing employment grew from 50 million to 120 million.' },
        { date: '2000s-2010s', event: 'China lifts 500+ million from poverty and becomes world\'s second-largest economy', context: 'University enrollment exploded 10x (1998-2020). China went from copying products to innovating: Huawei, Alibaba, Tencent, BYD became global competitors. GDP per capita rose from $1,000 (2001) to $10,000 (2019). The middle class grew to 400 million people.' },
        { date: '2020s', event: 'China faces the middle-income trap, aging population, and geopolitical tensions', context: 'GDP per capita reached $12,700 but growth slowed from 10% to 4-5%. The demographic dividend is reversing, China\'s population started shrinking in 2022. Youth unemployment hit 21% in 2023. U.S.-China tech restrictions and supply chain diversification ("friend-shoring") threaten the export model. Can China transition to innovation, or will it get trapped?' }
