    cq('What is the "resource curse" and why does it make resource-rich countries poorer?',
      ['It\'s a myth, natural resources always help a country grow, so any nation that finds oil is guaranteed to become wealthier over time','Resource wealth fuels corruption, appreciates the currency (killing manufacturing via Dutch Disease), and creates boom-bust dependence on volatile commodities, Nigeria earned $600B+ from oil but GDP per capita declined','Resources simply run out too quickly, so the country grows for a few years and then collapses once the deposits are fully depleted','Only small countries ever experience it, because large nations have economies diverse enough to absorb a sudden resource windfall safely'],
      1,'The resource curse explains why Nigeria, Venezuela, and DRC are poor despite massive resources, while South Korea, Japan, and Singapore are rich with almost none. Institutions matter more than endowments.'),
    cq('South Korea went from $158 GDP per capita (1960) to $35,000 (2024). What was the key to breaking through the middle-income trap?',
      ['A sudden oil discovery, since selling crude on world markets was what funded South Korea\'s leap from poverty to wealth','Massive foreign aid that simply transferred enough money from rich nations to push South Korea past the middle-income line','Investing in education (4-5% of GDP), building export champions that competed globally, and developing strong institutions, transitioning from manufacturing to innovation','Low taxes and deregulation alone, since cutting government rules was the single change that triggered South Korea\'s rise'],
      2,'South Korea invested heavily in human capital and forced its companies (Samsung, Hyundai) to compete globally. The combination of education + export discipline + strong institutions enabled the transition from manufacturing to innovation.'),
    cq('India\'s median age is 28 (vs. China\'s 39, Japan\'s 49). Why is this a potential economic advantage?',
      ['Younger people are simply smarter than older people, so a low median age automatically raises a country\'s economic output','India has a massive demographic dividend, a huge working-age population relative to dependents. If educated and employed, this workforce could drive decades of high growth, similar to China\'s 1980-2015 transformation','Younger populations consume more goods, and that extra spending alone is enough to guarantee fast and lasting GDP growth','It is not an advantage at all, because older populations have saved more and a richer, older country always grows faster'],
      1,'The demographic dividend provides a one-time growth window when workers vastly outnumber dependents. China rode this wave for 35 years. India\'s 900 million people under 45 represent the world\'s largest untapped demographic dividend.'),
    cq('Norway has $1.6 trillion in its sovereign wealth fund from oil profits. Why did Norway succeed where Nigeria failed?',
      ['Norway simply had more oil than Nigeria, so the larger reserves were what allowed it to grow rich while Nigeria stayed poor','Norway\'s strong pre-existing institutions (democracy, rule of law, low corruption) allowed it to save and invest oil revenue rather than let it fuel corruption and Dutch Disease. Institutions determine whether resources are a blessing or curse.','Norway discovered its oil decades earlier than Nigeria, and that head start alone explains the enormous gap in their outcomes','The answer is purely geographical, colder countries always develop better, so Norway was destined to succeed regardless of policy'],
      1,'Norway already had strong democratic institutions, rule of law, and low corruption before oil was discovered. This allowed it to create the Government Pension Fund and invest wisely. Nigeria lacked these institutional safeguards, so oil wealth fueled corruption instead of development.'),
    cq('What is the middle-income trap and which country is the most famous example?',
      ['When a country cannot grow past $1,000 GDP per capita, with Somalia as the classic example of an economy frozen at the very bottom','When a country reaches middle income through cheap labor but can\'t transition to innovation-driven growth. Argentina has been trapped for 100+ years, it was one of the world\'s richest countries in 1900 and remains middle-income today.','When a country\'s middle class shrinks until it disappears, with the United States often cited as the most prominent recent example','When a country\'s government debt grows larger than its annual GDP, with Japan held up as the textbook case of the trap'],
      1,'Argentina is the textbook middle-income trap case: wealthy in 1900, still middle-income in 2025. The trap catches countries that rely on manufacturing/commodities but fail to invest in education, R&D, and institutions needed for innovation-driven growth.')
