      {label:'Cut interest rates and increase spending to boost growth to 4%, worry about inflation later',outcome:'With inflation at 6%, cutting rates and increasing spending would pour gasoline on the fire. The 1970s proved that ignoring inflation leads to stagflation, high inflation AND stagnation. Inflation always needs to be addressed first because it erodes wages, savings, and economic stability for everyone.',correct:false},
      {label:'Prioritize inflation: maintain tight monetary policy (high rates) to bring inflation to 2%, use targeted fiscal support for the most affected populations, and address debt gradually through growth-oriented reforms',outcome:'This is the textbook response and what most successful economies have done. Paul Volcker crushed 13% inflation with high rates in 1980-1982, it caused a recession but set the stage for 20 years of growth. Inflation is the #1 priority because it\'s a hidden tax on everyone, especially the poor. Debt is a long-term problem best addressed through growth, not austerity during a challenging period.',correct:true},
      {label:'Slash government spending by 30% to reduce the debt from 120% to 80% of GDP as fast as possible',outcome:'Austerity during economic stress is counterproductive. Greece tried this after 2010: GDP fell 25%, unemployment hit 27%, and the debt-to-GDP ratio actually INCREASED because the economy shrank faster than the debt. The UK\'s post-2010 austerity slowed its recovery by years compared to the U.S. Timing matters, aggressive debt reduction is best done during expansions, not during periods of economic stress.',correct:false}
