      {label:'Targeted tariffs on strategic sectors (semiconductors, advanced technology, critical minerals) combined with investment in domestic capacity make economic sense. Blanket tariffs on all goods would raise consumer prices, trigger retaliation, and harm U.S. industries that depend on Chinese inputs.',outcome:'This is the nuanced economic position. Protecting strategic industries (semiconductors, rare earths) has national security justification. But tariffing consumer goods just raises prices for American families. The CHIPS Act model, targeted protection plus domestic investment, is more effective than blanket protectionism.',correct:true},
      {label:'Fully oppose, free trade should have no exceptions, even for national security',outcome:'Pure free trade maximizes economic efficiency, but ignoring national security risks is naive. Dependence on a strategic rival for critical technologies (chips, rare earths, pharmaceuticals) creates unacceptable vulnerability. The economic case for free trade must be balanced against security concerns.',correct:false}
