      { takeaway: 'Tariffs are paid by domestic consumers, not foreign exporters', detail: 'The biggest misconception in trade policy: tariffs are an import tax paid by the importing country\'s businesses and passed to consumers. U.S.-China tariffs cost the average household $831/year. China receives the same pre-tariff price.' },
      { takeaway: 'Trade wars create concentrated winners and diffuse losers', detail: 'Steel tariffs saved 12,000 jobs but threatened 200,000+ in steel-using industries. Farm retaliation cost $27 billion in lost exports and required $28 billion in bailouts. The math of blanket protectionism rarely works.' },
      { takeaway: 'Strategic tariffs differ from blanket protectionism', detail: 'Protecting semiconductors for national security is different from tariffing consumer goods. The CHIPS Act model (targeted protection + domestic investment) is more effective than Smoot-Hawley-style blanket tariffs. The line between security and protectionism is the central debate.' }
