      {label:'Block the default agreement and require a choice screen, let users actively select their preferred search engine when setting up a new device',outcome:'This is the competitive solution. Android already requires choice screens in Europe (EU antitrust ruling). When users get a genuine choice, Google still wins most of the time (its product is good), but competitors gain 10-15% share, enough to sustain competition and innovation. The goal isn\'t to punish Google but to ensure competition.',correct:true},
      {label:'Break up Google entirely, split search, advertising, YouTube, and cloud into separate companies',outcome:'Breaking up Google is the most extreme option and may not solve the problem. Google\'s search monopoly comes from data advantages, not from bundling with other products. A standalone Google Search would still have 90%+ share due to network effects. Structural separation is a blunt tool that could reduce efficiency without improving competition in search.',correct:false}
