    cq('What are "network effects" and why do they create monopolies?',
      ['When computers connect to networks','A network effect occurs when a product becomes more valuable as more people use it. This creates a positive feedback loop (more users → better product → more users) that tends toward monopoly because latecomers can\'t replicate the established network. That\'s why Google, Facebook, and Amazon dominate.','A type of government regulation','When companies merge together'],
      1,'Network effects explain why digital markets concentrate far more than traditional markets. Once a platform reaches critical mass, the feedback loop makes it nearly impossible for competitors to catch up, you\'d need everyone to switch simultaneously.'),
    cq('Why is "zero marginal cost" revolutionary for platform economics?',
      ['It means companies don\'t have to pay employees','The cost of serving one additional digital user is essentially zero, unlike physical products where each unit costs materials and labor. This means platforms can scale to billions of users with minimal incremental cost, creating profit margins impossible in the physical economy.','It means everything is free','It doesn\'t affect economics'],
      1,'Zero marginal cost breaks traditional economics. A car company spending $25K per vehicle can\'t easily scale to billions. But Google serving one more search costs essentially nothing. This enables massive scaling and explains why digital platforms achieve profit margins of 30-40% vs. 5-10% for physical businesses.'),
    cq('How do "free" platforms like Google and Meta actually make money?',
      ['They don\'t, they\'re charities','They collect user data and attention, then sell targeted advertising. Meta earns ~$55/U.S. user/year; Google earns ~$280. The service is free because you pay with data and attention, "if the product is free, you\'re the product."','Government subsidies','They charge hidden fees'],
      1,'The business model is: free service → data collection → behavioral profiling → targeted advertising. U.S. digital ad spending exceeds $250 billion/year. Users "pay" with their data and attention, which platforms sell to advertisers at premium prices.'),
    cq('Why is regulating Big Tech harder than regulating traditional monopolies?',
      ['Big Tech is too powerful to regulate','Traditional antitrust focused on consumer harm through HIGH PRICES. But platforms like Google and Facebook are FREE, making it hard to show consumer harm in the traditional sense. New frameworks must address harm through reduced privacy, competition, and innovation rather than high prices.','It\'s not harder, the same rules apply','Big Tech doesn\'t have monopoly power'],
      1,'Traditional antitrust assumes monopolies charge high prices. But Google charges nothing for search. The challenge is proving harm through reduced competition, privacy erosion, and innovation stifling rather than price gouging. This requires new legal and economic frameworks.'),
    cq('What is a "killer acquisition" and why is it concerning?',
      ['When a company acquires a killer product','When a dominant company buys a potential competitor to prevent it from becoming a threat, like Facebook buying Instagram ($1B) and WhatsApp ($19B). These acquisitions eliminated credible competitors, maintaining Meta\'s monopoly. The FTC argues they should have been blocked.','A hostile takeover','When a small company acquires a large one'],
      1,'Killer acquisitions let dominant platforms buy emerging competitors before they become threats. Facebook bought Instagram when it had 30M users and could have become a rival. By acquiring it, Meta eliminated competition and grew to 3B+ users. This pattern, buy potential competitors rather than compete with them, is central to the Big Tech antitrust debate.')
