        { date: '2009', event: 'Greece reveals its debt-to-GDP is 127%, much higher than previously reported', context: 'Greece had been hiding the true size of its deficits. When the truth emerged, investors lost confidence. Greek bond yields spiked from 5% to 30%, the market was pricing in default risk. Greece couldn\'t borrow at affordable rates.' },
        { date: '2010-2015', event: 'EU and IMF impose severe austerity in exchange for bailouts', context: 'Government spending was cut 30%. Pensions slashed 40%. Public sector salaries cut 25%. VAT raised to 24%. The goal: reduce deficits and restore confidence. The result: GDP fell 25%, the worst peacetime economic collapse in modern European history.' },
        { date: '2020', event: 'U.S. debt-to-GDP hits 129% (higher than Greece\'s crisis level), but NO crisis occurs', context: 'The U.S. borrowed $5 trillion for COVID stimulus, pushing debt above Greece\'s crisis threshold. Yet bond yields barely rose. Why? The U.S. has the world\'s reserve currency, the Fed can buy bonds (QE), and global investors view Treasuries as the safest asset on earth. The U.S. has "exorbitant privilege" that Greece never had.' },
        { date: '2024', event: 'U.S. debt reaches $34 trillion (123% debt-to-GDP), debate rages over sustainability', context: 'Interest costs hit $1 trillion/year. The CBO projects debt-to-GDP will reach 172% by 2054. But the U.S. still borrows at low rates. The question: does the U.S. have a genuine fiscal problem (interest costs crowding out other spending) or just a numbers-look-scary problem? The answer is probably somewhere in between.' }
