      {label:'Gradual fiscal consolidation, moderately reduce deficits through a combination of revenue increases (closing tax loopholes, modest rate increases on high earners) and spending reforms (healthcare cost control, efficiency improvements) over 10-15 years while investing in growth-enhancing areas',outcome:'This is the responsible approach. Gradual adjustment avoids recession while bending the debt trajectory. Revenue increases from closing loopholes (estimated $500B-$1T over 10 years) plus healthcare cost reform plus growth-enhancing investment can stabilize debt-to-GDP. The key: grow g (GDP growth) while reducing the primary deficit gradually.',correct:true},
      {label:'Ignore it, the U.S. can never have a debt crisis because of the dollar',outcome:'While the U.S. has enormous fiscal space, it\'s not infinite. Interest payments consuming 17% of revenue and rising crowd out other spending. The reserve currency provides a buffer but not immunity. Britain lost reserve currency status in the 1950s when it overextended fiscally. Complacency is dangerous.',correct:false}
